    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d515">

                <pb facs="0701" n="601" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg601"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">211.65/100: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Italy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Long</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Rome</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-04-03T11:00:00+01:00">April 3, 1934—11
                        a.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 3—8:30 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>51. Department’s telegram No. 14, March 10, 1 p.m. Matter taken up with Foreign
                    Office and I am now in receipt of reply from Under Secretary for Foreign Affairs
                    as follows: <pb facs="0702" n="602" xml:id="pg602"/>
                    <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>[“] As the Embassy knows the Royal Government in 1927 suggested that it
                            would be well to open negotiations for such a convention proposing that
                            they should be held in Rome and the Department of State courteously
                            agreed.</p>
                        <p>I trust, therefore, that it will still be agreeable to hold the
                            negotiations in Rome and in that connection I feel it might be well to
                            transmit to Your Excellency at this time the enclosed draft
                                convention.<anchor n="17" xml:id="d515-f1"/> This draft is upon the
                            same basis as the extradition treaties which the Royal Government has
                            concluded with other countries during the past few years and which have
                            proved entirely satisfactory.</p>
                        <p>In any event I shall be pleased to furnish Your Excellency with any other
                            information and explanations which may be thought useful.</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <p>Mail despatch follows. Please instruct.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Long</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
